# 18. RESPONSIVE DESIGN

Use 820px as the main responsive breakpoint.

At widths of 820px and below, reduce navigation padding to approximately `16px 18px`, hide the desktop centre menu, and provide a functional mobile menu. Keep the logo and contact action visible.

Adapt the hero headline so it wraps naturally. Clear desktop inline positioning values where necessary and use responsive sizing such as `clamp(44px, 6vw, 96px)`. Scale the featured monument and foreground cut-out proportionally. Position the HUD around 50% horizontal and 66% vertical, and move the caption toward the bottom with approximately 18px side spacing. Move the scroll hint to approximately 18px from the right and 24px from the bottom.

Reduce floating element sizes and adjust their positioning. Move the company promise heading to approximately 18px from the left and 11vh from the top. Position the glass card around 18px from the left and 6vh from the bottom with a width around 210px. Position the feature list around 18px from the right and 6vh from the bottom, with a width around 45vw. Reduce row typography and hide unnecessary arrows.

For the monument collection, use approximately `50px 18px 70px` padding, stack the three cards into one column, and make each card approximately 340px tall. Display the four service highlights in a two-column grid.

Do not simply shrink the desktop design. Adapt the camera composition, text placement, interaction targets, and visual hierarchy to mobile screens. Ensure the floating monument reveal remains functional on touch devices.

# 19. PERFORMANCE, ACCESSIBILITY, AND ANIMATION QUALITY

The website must remain smooth during scrolling, video scrubbing, pointer movement, and interactive monument transitions.

Use a single requestAnimationFrame loop for the main cinematic story. Avoid unnecessary React state updates on every frame. Use refs for high-frequency animation values and update styles through a controlled animation layer. Use passive scroll and pointer listeners where appropriate. Clean up event listeners, video callbacks, animation frames, and other resources when components unmount.

Cache video frames and avoid repeated live seeking. Reuse decoded image bitmaps where possible. Optimize images using WebP or AVIF where suitable. Preload only essential hero assets and lazy-load below-the-fold images. Prevent layout shifts and avoid loading unnecessary large assets.

Do not introduce heavy 3D libraries unless they are genuinely required. Prefer CSS transforms, perspective, JavaScript, canvas, and the existing frame-rendering architecture. If a minimal library is necessary for the required animation quality, explain why and keep dependencies limited.

Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`. Provide a less motion-intensive experience for visitors who request reduced motion. Make the floating monument, close/back control, hotspots, navigation, and cards keyboard accessible. Use semantic HTML, accessible button labels, visible focus states, and appropriate image alt text. Ensure text remains readable over imagery.

